\begin_layout Section
Experimental model updating: plucked tongue, tongue on its chamber, wind tunnel
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The finite elements fix what geometry can fix. What they cannot know (the real thickness to a hundredth of a millimetre, the damping, how much of the air swept by the tongue really goes through the chamber, the rest lift of the tip) must come from measurements on a real reed. The protocol follows one rule: the least certain parameters are calibrated on the most robust measurements, and the remaining observables are kept as predictions. A model fitted on everything can no longer be contradicted. Three gestures are made, in this order, each building on the previous one: (a) the tongue alone, plucked; (b) the tongue on its chamber, plucked; (c) the tongue played on the wind tunnel. The workshop version, step by step, is in docs/protocole_recalage_experimental.md.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Measurement chain
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A sound card and a microphone at 15 cm with a fixed gain, 48 kHz, mono WAV; the position of the microphone is marked and kept for the whole session. A phone is enough for a frequency but not for a damping, because its automatic gain lifts a decaying sound. Each file holds five plucks, and each configuration is recorded three times: the scatter between plucks measures the gesture, the scatter between files the mounting. The temperature is noted at the beginning and the end; for spring steel the modulus decreases by roughly 
\begin_inset Formula $2.5\times10^{-4}$
\end_inset
 per kelvin (an order of magnitude to be checked on the actual grade), so the frequency drops by about 0.2 cent per kelvin.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Analysis of a plucked sound: damped sinusoids, band by band
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For each pluck the analysis (banc_recherche/pince_modes.py) looks for spectral peaks on windows of 30 ms, 0.1, 0.3 and 1 s taken at the start of the decay: a heavily damped mode, such as the air resonator of a chamber with a quality factor of 20, has lost 40 dB within 25 ms and is invisible on a one-second window, while a lightly damped tongue mode is only resolved from its neighbours on the long one. A Blackman-Harris window (side lobes at 
\begin_inset Formula $-$
\end_inset
92 dB) keeps the side lobes of the first mode, often 60 dB above the others, from passing for modes. Around each candidate frequency 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{c}$
\end_inset
 the signal is filtered by a zero-phase Gaussian band, shifted to baseband, decimated, and a sum of four damped complex exponentials is fitted by ESPRIT 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
before ""
key "roy1989esprit"
literal "true"

\end_inset
, a subspace method of the same family as the matrix pencil 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
before ""
key "hua1990matrixpencil"
literal "true"

\end_inset
. The strongest component near the centre gives the frequency 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset
 and the decay rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset
, hence
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\zeta=\frac{\alpha}{\sqrt{\alpha^{2}+\omega_{d}^{2}}},\qquad Q=\frac{1}{2\zeta}.
\]

\end_inset

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The band filter does not bias the damping. If 
\begin_inset Formula $x(t)=A\,e^{st}$
\end_inset
 with 
\begin_inset Formula $s=-\alpha+\mathrm{i}\omega$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset
 is the impulse response of the filter, then once the filter has risen
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y(t)=\int g(\tau)\,x(t-\tau)\,\mathrm{d}\tau=A\,e^{st}\int g(\tau)\,e^{-s\tau}\,\mathrm{d}\tau=A\,\hat{G}(s)\,e^{st},
\]

\end_inset

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
the same exponential with another complex amplitude. The filter only removes the other modes and the noise outside the band; the first 
\begin_inset Formula $4\sigma_{t}$
\end_inset
 of the response, with 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{t}=1/(2\pi\sigma_{f})$
\end_inset
 for a Gaussian of standard deviation 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{f}$
\end_inset
, are skipped. The components found in the successive plucks are grouped by frequency (median and scatter). Components that do not decay, such as mains hum, are discarded. A tongue that moves in and out of its slot radiates non-linearly, so the harmonics 
\begin_inset Formula $kf_{1}$
\end_inset
 of the first mode are present in the sound without being modes; they are recognised by their frequency (within 0.3 per cent of 
\begin_inset Formula $kf_{1}$
\end_inset
) and confirmed by their decay, 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
 times faster than the first mode, since the 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
-th power of a decaying amplitude decays 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
 times faster.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
On synthetic plucks built with the Elmer modes of the bass tongue (Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:pince"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset
), with a second harmonic, mains hum and noise, the frequencies are recovered within 
\begin_inset Formula $10^{-4}$
\end_inset
, the damping ratios within 3 per cent, and an air resonator with 
\begin_inset Formula $Q=20$
\end_inset
 within 5 per cent; the harmonic is labelled as such and the hum is not reported. These tests are part of the repository (tests/test_pince_modes.py).
\end_layout

