.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
Self-sustained conditions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A plucked reed rings and dies. A blown reed rings and goes on. This chapter is about the difference: where the energy of the note is kept, where it leaks away, and what decides that the gain wins. It answers Q5, and on the way it gives what this part can say about Q3 (the least energy state) and Q4 (the bellows).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Stored energy
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Think of a child on a swing. At the top of each swing the child is still, and all the energy is in the height. At the bottom the child goes fastest, and the energy is in the speed. The energy passes from one form to the other, twice per period. A vibrating tongue does the same. Its energy is shared between motion and bending:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E=\tfrac{1}{2}\,m\,\dot{y}^{2}+\tfrac{1}{2}\,k\,y^{2}
\]

\end_inset

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset
 is the displacement of the tip, 
\begin_inset Formula $\dot{y}$
\end_inset
 its velocity, and 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
 the modal mass and stiffness of the first mode reduced to the tip, so that the tongue behaves like a single mass on a spring (Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:rk4"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset
 gives them for five tongues). At the end of each swing all the energy is in the bending; at the middle of the slot, all of it is in the motion.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A swing left alone slows down, and so does a tongue. With a damping ratio 
\begin_inset Formula $\zeta$
\end_inset
, the energy left after one cycle is multiplied by about 
\begin_inset Formula $e^{-4\pi\zeta}$
\end_inset
: the tongue loses a fraction of about 
\begin_inset Formula $4\pi\zeta$
\end_inset
 of its energy at each cycle. With the value used in the models, 
\begin_inset Formula $\zeta=0.004$
\end_inset
 (a guessed value), this is 5 per cent per cycle. A steady note therefore asks the air to give back about 5 per cent of the stored energy at every cycle: 440 times per second for an A4.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
How does the air give energy? The force of the air on the tongue does work as the tongue moves: over one cycle, the work is 
\begin_inset Formula $W=\oint F\,\mathrm{d}y=\oint F\,\dot{y}\,\mathrm{d}t$
\end_inset
. Split the force in two parts. The part that follows the displacement 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset
 only makes the spring stiffer or softer: it changes the pitch, and over a full cycle it gives nothing. Only the part that follows the velocity 
\begin_inset Formula $\dot{y}$
\end_inset
 does work. This is the exact meaning of the push on the swing given at the right moment. The flow computation of the repository (research/cfd/sandwich2d) computes precisely these two parts on a tongue forced to move: the work of the air per cycle, and the damping and stiffness that the air adds to the steel.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The air around the tongue also stores energy, for a short time. The air of the chamber is a spring: its compliance 
\begin_inset Formula $C=V/(\gamma P_{\mathrm{atm}})$
\end_inset
, for a volume 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset
, the atmospheric pressure 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{\mathrm{atm}}$
\end_inset
 and the ratio of specific heats 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset
, stores 
\begin_inset Formula $\tfrac{1}{2}Cp^{2}$
\end_inset
 when the pressure in it varies by 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset
. The air in the holes is a mass: its inertance 
\begin_inset Formula $L=\rho\ell/S$
\end_inset
, for a hole of length 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell$
\end_inset
 and section 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset
, stores 
\begin_inset Formula $\tfrac{1}{2}Lq^{2}$
\end_inset
 when a flow 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset
 goes through it. These reservoirs take energy and give it back within each cycle. They do not create energy; they shift it in time. And shifting the force in time is exactly what can turn a force that follows the displacement into a force that follows the velocity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Back to Q3. On the limit cycle, the stored energy does not rise or fall from one cycle to the next: the gain balances the loss. The sounding reed sits where the books balance, not where the energy is lowest. Whether, among the possible regimes, it settles in the one that wastes the least would need the efficiency of each regime, and the models do not predict efficiency yet (Section Intensity).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Dissipation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Where does the energy go? Follow a puff of air through a note.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Into the steel and its rivet. The tongue rubs inside itself and against its clamp. This is the damping 
\begin_inset Formula $\zeta$
\end_inset
 measured on a plucked tongue (Section Material of reeds).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Into the gap. Around the tongue, in the slot, the air is sticky: in a gap of 30 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset
m, 80 per cent of the pressure drop is viscous (Section The reed socket).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Into the jets. At each sharp hole, in the soundboard and under the pallet, the air leaves as a jet and loses its speed in turbulence. The models write this loss as 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta p=\rho\,q|q|/\left(2(C_{d}S)^{2}\right)$
\end_inset
 for a hole of section 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset
 and discharge coefficient 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{d}$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Into the source. A bellows or a turbine that gives less flow when the pressure rises takes part of the energy back (question Q4).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Into the sound. A small opening in a flat baffle radiates the power 
\begin_inset Formula $P=\rho\,\omega^{2}Q^{2}/(4\pi c)$
\end_inset
, where 
\begin_inset Formula $Q$
\end_inset
 is the amplitude of the oscillating flow, 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega$
\end_inset
 its angular frequency, 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset
 the density of air and 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset
 the speed of sound. The code of the repository recovers this formula within 0.01 per cent on a known sinusoidal flow (
\begin_inset Formula $2.1277\cdot10^{-5}$
\end_inset
 W against 
\begin_inset Formula $2.1279\cdot10^{-5}$
\end_inset
 W). The sound is the purpose of the instrument, but it is a small part of the budget.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Which of these losses sets the starting pressure? In the single-reed model, the starting pressure grows like 
\begin_inset Formula $\zeta^{2}$
\end_inset
. One could hope to fix the too-low thresholds of the bass (1.8 Pa instead of something near 100 Pa, Section A physical model for reed design) with more damping. But it would take 
\begin_inset Formula $Q=12$
\end_inset
, whereas a plucked reed rings for several seconds. The damping of the tongue cannot be what is missing. The missing piece is in the motor, the push, not in the brakes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A warning belongs here. An early version of the model said that the flow dissipated fifteen times more than the tongue: 
\begin_inset Formula $-40$
\end_inset
 s
\begin_inset Formula $^{-1}$
\end_inset
 against 
\begin_inset Formula $-2.6$
\end_inset
 s
\begin_inset Formula $^{-1}$
\end_inset
 for the tongue alone. These 40 s
\begin_inset Formula $^{-1}$
\end_inset
 came from the swept-volume term that should not be there, and a test protected the number. The true share of each loss is not known yet.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Back to Q4. The source is part of the losses, and its place in the circuit matters. In one version of the single-reed model, a softer source raised the starting pressure (23.0 Pa with an ideal flow source, 25.8 Pa with 
\begin_inset Formula $R=2\cdot10^{8}$
\end_inset
 Pa
\begin_inset Formula $\cdot$
\end_inset
s/m
\begin_inset Formula $^{3}$
\end_inset
, 35.9 Pa with 
\begin_inset Formula $R=5\cdot10^{7}$
\end_inset
) and left the choking pressure unchanged, because choking is set by the geometry of the opening. These numbers come from the version with the swept-volume term and must be computed again. In the two-reed network, a resistance shared at the pallet locked two reeds together, while one resistance per chamber let them beat (Section Multi reed coupling). The internal impedance of the real source must be measured before any of these numbers can be trusted (experiment E1).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Multi reed coupling
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Play a musette chord. Two or three reeds sound each note, tuned a little apart, and their beating gives the shimmer of the musette. The reeds of one note take their air from the same bellows and give it back through the same pallet. They share their air, and they talk through it. Do they pull each other towards unison?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The two-reed network of the repository answers for each version of the model. Its elements are two tongues, two chambers, two holes in the soundboard, the common channel under the pallet, and the pallet. In the version where the start was obtained with a tuned resistance, the place of this resistance decided everything. Shared at the pallet, it locked the two reeds to unison up to 80 cents apart (4.9 Hz): no real musette does that. One resistance per chamber let them beat freely: 1.209 Hz of beating for two blades 1.21 Hz apart (20 cents). Near unison the voices still pulled each other: the beat was 27 per cent slower than the difference of the blades at 1 cent, 20 per cent at 2 cents, 12 per cent at 3 cents, and not slower at all at 5 cents (low A, 300 Pa). The air did not double: two identical reeds took 1.56 times the air of one. And two reeds in phase radiated better than one, with an efficiency of 
\begin_inset Formula $2.9\cdot10^{-5}$
\end_inset
 against 
\begin_inset Formula $1.5\cdot10^{-5}$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the version with a closing opening, which starts without any tuned resistance, two A4 reeds at 1000 Pa lock up to about 2 cents apart, the beat is 12 per cent slower at 5 cents, and they are free at 20 cents. There, the air nearly doubles with two reeds (4 per cent less than double).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The versions disagree on the numbers, and on the air. They agree on the shape: the voices pull each other near unison, and the pull vanishes beyond a few cents. A musette tuned some tens of cents apart should beat freely. The test is simple and cheap: tune one reed while the other is blocked, then release both and read the beat at the tuner (experiment E3 of the two-reed audit).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One warning on the data. The recordings of beating reeds kept in the repository since 2021 (beat-01 to beat-17) are a musette played on a sampled instrument found online, not a real accordion. They cannot test the coupling.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): experiment E3 on two or three notes of a real instrument: each reed tuned alone (the other blocked with tape or paper), then both together, at two pressures read on the water manometer.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Harmonic Saturation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Play a note softly: it is round. Play it loud: it grows bright, richer in high partials. Push harder still, and at some point it chokes. Two things saturate along the way: the amplitude of the tongue, and the richness of the sound.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The harmonics are made by the chopping of the flow (Section Inharmonicity in reeds). The more abruptly the tongue closes and opens the passage, the richer the sound. Measuring this richness looks easy and hides two traps, both met while studying the model. The first trap is to measure the wrong quantity: the sound of a free reed is radiated by the modulated flow, and far away it follows 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{d}q/\mathrm{d}t$
\end_inset
, not the pressure in the chamber. The second trap is to take the strongest peak of the spectrum as the fundamental. Differentiating the flow raises the high partials by 6 dB per octave, and the strongest peak had moved to the fourth harmonic, so every harmonic was counted from the wrong frequency. Counted from the true fundamental (120 Hz) on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{d}q/\mathrm{d}t$
\end_inset
, one version of the model gave twelve harmonics above 
\begin_inset Formula $-30$
\end_inset
 dB: a rich timbre. This count was made with the version that still had the swept-volume term; it must be made again with the corrected model, and compared with a real reed (experiment E12).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The amplitude must saturate too, and in the corrected single-reed model it does not: the travel of the tip keeps growing with the flow (Section A physical model for reed design). A real tongue is held back by something the model lacks. The best candidate is a force that depends on the position of the tongue: once out of its slot, the tongue sits in the jet and no longer feels the full pressure drop.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The last saturation is choking. When the pressure is too high, the tongue is blown out of its slot and stays out. The opening then no longer changes with the position of the tongue: 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial h/\partial y=0$
\end_inset
. The valve stops modulating, the push disappears, and the note dies. The corrected model shows this cleanly: above the band of oscillation, the growth rate falls exactly to 
\begin_inset Formula $-\zeta\omega_{1}$
\end_inset
, the damping of the tongue alone, as if the air were no longer there. In the earlier version with the swept-volume term, the band ran from 23.0 to 379.8 Pa; the model gave the choking pressure without being asked for it. With the corrected model, this band must be computed again, and compared with the bench (experiment E4).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Back to Q6. How the brightness follows the loudness is the control law of an instrument: it is what makes a physical model playable in continuous nuances, instead of switching between recorded layers. It is measured as a table of notes by nuances, pushing and pulling (experiment E11).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the spectrum of one isolated reed at three nuances (pp, mf, ff), close microphone, pushing and pulling (experiments E11 and E12), and the pressure at which it chokes (E4).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Hysteresis vs self sustained conditions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Reeds are said to need more pressure to start than to keep sounding. If this is true, a reed has a memory: between two pressures, it can be silent or sounding, and which one depends on its past. This section explains what this memory means for the physics, and how to test it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When the rest of the tongue becomes unstable, the oscillation can be born in two ways. In the gentle way, called supercritical, the amplitude grows from zero, continuously, as the pressure passes the threshold; near the threshold the square of the amplitude grows in proportion to the distance to the threshold (the normal form of Stuart and Landau). There is no memory. In the abrupt way, called subcritical, the amplitude jumps to a finite value at the threshold. Coming down, the reed keeps sounding below 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\mathrm{on}}$
\end_inset
, and stops only at 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\mathrm{off}}<p_{\mathrm{on}}$
\end_inset
. Between the two, the silent reed and the sounding reed both exist: the system is bistable, and it stays in the state where it is.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
How can the two be told apart? Only by going up and coming down. The model showed why. A first study concluded that the bifurcation was supercritical, after two errors. The state of the tongue, described by several modes, had been rebuilt from the position of the tip alone, which is not enough information, and every restart died out. And the amplitude followed the square-root law above the threshold (successive ratios of 1.35, 1.29, 1.35 and 1.45, as expected), which seemed to settle the question. But a weakly subcritical bifurcation follows the same law above its threshold. The law is necessary, not sufficient. Starting from an established oscillation and lowering the flow settled it (Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:hysteresis-modele"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset
): the reed kept sounding down to 0.82 times the starting flow. The model gave 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\mathrm{on}}=25.76$
\end_inset
 Pa and 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\mathrm{off}}\approx19.44$
\end_inset
 Pa, a ratio of about 1.33. The bifurcation is subcritical.
\end_layout

