\begin_layout Standard
Two instruments cost nothing. A gasometer is a box turned upside down and floating in a bucket of water: filled with air, it feeds the instrument and sinks as the air leaks out, and for the box of the workshop a descent of 6.7 cm per minute is 1 litre per minute. A U-tube of transparent hose half filled with water and a ruler is a manometer: 1 mm of water is about 10 Pa (9.81 Pa exactly). With these two, the criteria above can be checked on any instrument.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
What a leak does to the music is known in the workshop and explained by the network. A leak in parallel with the tongues takes flow from a source of finite resistance, so the pressure falls before the note: the attack is soft, and a pianissimo starts badly. A leaking valve lets air through the slot of the silent tongue of its plate, and if that tongue is close to its threshold it sounds faintly: the ghost note.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Acoustic transmittance of valves
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A closed valve, a closed pallet, a mute: each is a thin wall between a cavity full of sound and the outside. How much sound passes through it is its transmittance, usually written as a transmission loss in decibels. Nothing in this work has measured one yet; the method and the software are ready, and they are cheap.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The rigorous way is the transfer matrix method with four microphones (standard ASTM E2611): a tube, the sample in the middle, two microphones upstream and two downstream, and two different terminations so that the four coefficients of the matrix can be separated. The transmission loss follows from the matrix as 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{TL}=20\log_{10}\left(|T_{11}+T_{12}/\rho c+\rho c\,T_{21}+T_{22}|/2\right)$
\end_inset

 for a tube of constant section and an anechoic end, where 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{11}$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{22}$
\end_inset

 are the four coefficients and 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho c$
\end_inset

 the characteristic impedance of air (banc_recherche/transfer.py, transfer_matrix_two_load and tl_from_matrix). The cheap way uses the two microphones of the impedance tube and compares the spectra upstream and downstream, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{TL}=10\log_{10}(S_{\mathrm{in}}/S_{\mathrm{out}})$
\end_inset

, a coarse estimate, honest about being one. The sound card has three channels; the four-microphone method would need a second pass with the microphones moved, or a second card.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One thing the models already say about the valve is not about transmittance but about pitch, and it deserves a place here because it is surprising. In the audit of the one-reed model, an opening symmetric in the position of the tongue, 
\begin_inset Formula $S=b|y|$
\end_inset

, modulated the slot twice per period and the model played 470 Hz for a tongue of 220 Hz: an octave wrong. The flow through a free reed is one-way, and in the reading of the models it is the valve that makes it so. Whether the real tongue would behave like that without its valve is a question for the sandwich experiments, where a tongue without any valve plays in both directions; the point stands that the valve is not an accessory of the plate but part of what fixes the note.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the transmission loss of one leather valve and one plastic valve glued on a plate, in a tube with two microphones, by the coarse method first. A difference of a few decibels between the two materials would already be a result.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Sealing material influence of sound radiated
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Leather or plastic for the valves, felt or foam for the gaskets, wax or leather under the plates: the sealing materials are chosen by the hand and judged by the ear. Their influence on the radiated sound is of two kinds, and the second is the one usually forgotten.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first kind is what the material lets through when it is meant to seal. A leak is a place where air escapes without making a note, and it is not silent: a jet through a small orifice under pressure becomes turbulent and radiates a broadband hiss that rises high in frequency, into the ultrasound. In a recording, the hiss of a leak is rich where the voice of the tongue and the noise of the room are poor, above a few kilohertz. This is the handle by which the appendix on leak detection catches it: filter high, take the envelope of the hiss, and, if the pressure of the bellows is modulated at a known slow frequency, keep only what blinks at that frequency. The bellows of the bench, which already makes controlled strokes at 2 to 10 Hz, is the modulator; the synchronous detection is a few lines of code (banc_recherche/leak.py, lockin and leak_map); a microphone walked over the surface gives a map. None of this has yet been run on a real instrument.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The second kind is what the material does when it moves. A valve lifts and falls at every change of direction of the bellows; it has a mass and it slaps. The models ignore it, and the tuner of this work detects the reversal of the bellows by a sharp and isolated dip of the signal without silence, which is where the slap lives. A stiff plastic valve and a supple leather one will not fall the same way, and the attack of the note after the reversal carries the difference. Nothing here has measured it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the same note recorded with its leather valve, then with a plastic one, at the same bellows pressure, with the tuner exporting the WAV; compare the spectra above 4 kHz during the held note (the hiss) and the first 50 ms after a reversal (the slap). Which note, and which instrument?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Dynamics of keyboard
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A key lifts a pallet. The pallet is the last door of the air road, and in the network it is an orifice: a mass of air 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho\ell/S$
\end_inset

 and a loss, with guessed dimensions of 3 cm
\begin_inset Formula $^{2}$
\end_inset

 of area, 10 mm of effective length and a lift of about 4 mm. On the bench it is an axis in degrees, swept from 2 to 22 degrees in the 2020 plan with a lever of 20 mm, or replaced by a fixed pallet and a timed electrovalve when only the arrival of the pressure matters. The keyboard of the bench is a matrix of electromagnets on the buttons, 24 on the left hand and 47 on the right, held with a reduced current once pressed; the firmware that drives it is written for an ESP32-S3 and has not yet been tested on hardware.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
What the speed of the key does to the note is a question of pressure rise. In the simulations the pressure of the bellows rises linearly in 20 ms, a hypothesis with no measurement behind it; a slow key gives the tongue a slow rise and a soft attack, a fast key a step. The response time of the note, from the opening to the steady sound, is already measured by the analysis chain of the research bench (the quantity called Tresp, computed with Praat 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
before ""
key "boersma_praat"
literal "true"

\end_inset
 through Parselmouth 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
before ""
key "jadoul2018parselmouth"
literal "true"

\end_inset
); what is missing is the position of the key against time next to it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There is a subtler point, and it is a prediction. In the version of the two-reed network with the closing section, the mass of air of the road above the tongue, hole and pallet together, is part of the mechanism of start-up: with that mass divided by ten the tongue does not start, with it multiplied by three it starts less well, and there is an optimum. A pallet barely open is a long thin passage and a large mass of air; a pallet wide open a small one. If the prediction holds, the depth of the key changes the ease of the start, not only the loudness. Experiment E2 of the audit is designed to see it: the starting pressure, read on the U-tube, against the lift of the pallet.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the force and travel of one key, with a load cell (HX711) and a small magnetic encoder (AS5600) on the bench, a new channel of the firmware proposed in the measurement map but not built. And experiment E2: the starting pressure of one tongue for five lifts of its pallet, set with shims. Is there an optimum, as the model says?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Pedale avec enclenchement manuel de notes type stylo bille
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The title describes a device in French: a pedal, or a latch worked by the hand, that holds a note open the way the button of a ballpoint pen holds its tip out, one press to engage and one to release. The musical purpose is a drone, a note that goes on sounding while the fingers are free. The design is Ewen's and is not in the repository; what is here is what the physics of the previous chapters says about such a note.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A latched pallet is a constant opening. The tongue behind it then depends on one thing only, the pressure of the bellows, and that pressure is shared with every other note being played. The audit of the one-reed model takes as its working hypothesis that the bellows is a source of flow with a finite internal resistance, not a source of pressure, and gives the proof in the playing: at a constant speed of the bellows, opening a chord makes the pressure fall, where a source of pressure would hold it and double the flow. A drone therefore costs pressure for the melody, and the cost is the flow of the drone tongue times the resistance of the source. The flows that the models give per tongue (0.1 to 0.4 litre per second at 1000 Pa) are known to be too large; the real one is read on the flow meter of the bench in a minute.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The second thing the physics says is about the two directions. A latched note on a plate with two tongues sounds one tongue on the push and the other on the draw, and the two tongues of a plate are two different blades: on Ewen's own instrument the tuner found differences of 15 to 25 cents between push and draw on the same notes. A drone that must stay in tune through the reversal of the bellows asks for the two tongues of its plate to be tuned together more carefully than the melody does.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the mechanism itself, and its question for the bench: how much flow the drone tongue takes at playing pressure, and by how much the pressure available to the other notes falls when it is latched, measured with the flow meter and the pressure sensor, or the U-tube.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Bend en bout de course du clavier
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The second French title names a key with extra travel: pressed to the bottom of its ordinary stroke the key opens the pallet, and pressed further it does something more, which bends the note. What can that something be? The models of this work name three levers, in the subsection on bend techniques above, and here they are weighed for a key.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The key cannot change the pressure of the bellows; that lever belongs to the arm. It can change the opening of the pallet: beyond the ordinary stroke it may close the pallet partly again, or open a second, smaller passage. In the two-reed network this changes the mass of air of the road above the tongue, which the model places in the mechanism of start-up, and the pitch effect of it is not known: it may be below what the ear notices. The key can also change the chamber: a second pallet at the end of the stroke could open a cell beside the chamber, changing its volume and its resonance. Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:fenetre"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset
 says that this lever is strong only when the chamber resonance is close to the note, within a ratio of 1.1 or so, and that such a chamber is hard to blow: 1322 Pa of threshold against 208 Pa at a ratio of 1.3, for the same blade in the plan. A bend at the end of the stroke is therefore, in the model, a trade between the depth of the bend and the ease of the note, and only a measurement will say where the trade is worth it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the mechanism you have in mind, and one measurement before building it: with modelling clay, reduce the volume of one chamber in two or three steps and read the pitch of its tongue with the tuner at a fixed pressure of the staircase. If the pitch moves by less than a few cents, the chamber is not the lever for that note, and the pallet is the one to try.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part
Reedblock to {Soundboard+box} coupling
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The block does not float. It stands on the soundboard, and the soundboard is a wall of the box. Whatever the tongue does to the air, the air does to these pieces of wood, and they answer. This part has one chapter, and it is mostly a list of what has not been measured yet, with the one thing that has.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
Soundboard
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The soundboard is the plate on which the block stands and in which the holes of the chambers are drilled; the pallets lie on its other face. In every model of this work it is rigid, and it enters only by its holes. That is already a great deal: the hole is the part of the chamber that the tongue feels most. Its effective length, computed by Elmer from the impedance seen from the slot, is 25.8 mm for chamber 1, 22.7 mm for chamber 6 and 30.4 mm for chamber 12, against a real thickness of 8 mm in the drawing. Two thirds of what the tongue feels as the mass of the hole is the air at its two ends, and the pallet, when it is close to the hole, lengthens it further; the finite element model takes the pallet far away, and this is a parameter to sweep.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A small sweep on chamber 1 shows how much the hole weighs. Keeping the cells and changing only the hole, the first resonance goes from 665 Hz (hole 4 by 12.5 mm, soundboard 8 mm, large outer correction) to 1314 Hz (hole 8 by 25 mm, soundboard 4 mm, small correction). A factor of four in area and a factor of two in thickness move the resonance by an octave; the volume of the chamber, which is what the maker usually thinks of first, was not touched.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The soundboard as a vibrating plate is absent from this work, and so is the box. The hypothesis of the network that the pallet is the only radiator is incomplete, as the audit says: the bellows, the box and the plate radiate too. How much, and at which frequencies, is a measurement that costs an impact hammer (a small screwdriver handle will do), a piezoelectric accelerometer of a few euros glued on the soundboard, and the third channel of the sound card: the frequency response from the tap to the acceleration gives the modes of the plate, their frequencies and their damping, with the same estimators the research bench already uses for tongues (the inter-spectrum of excitation.py, the ring-down and the matrix pencil 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
before ""
key "hua1990matrixpencil"
literal "true"

\end_inset
).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the frequency response of the soundboard of one instrument, block mounted and block removed, by hammer and accelerometer. The question to answer is simple: does any mode of the plate fall within a few per cent of a note of the instrument, or of the resonance of a chamber? If none does, the rigid soundboard of the models is a fair assumption for that instrument.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part
Other parts involved
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The bellows that breathes, the grille that the sound crosses, the box that holds it all, the pallets and the mutes: the rest of the instrument. Each one appears in this work as an assumption of the models, and this part says which assumption, and what would replace it by a measurement.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
Bellows
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The bellows is the lung. Everything upstream of the tongue is in it: the pressure, its rise, its fall, its reversal. The models of this work treat it in the simplest ways, and the bench replaces it by a motor.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Framed bellow
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A framed bellows is folds of cardboard and cloth, leather at the corners, and a wooden frame at each end that takes the block and the box. The workshop knows where it leaks: at the corners and at the frame. The proposed criterion of the booklet is less than 1 litre per minute at 500 Pa for the bellows alone, closed on a board, which by Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:trou"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset
 is the equivalent of a round hole a little over 1 mm across.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
What is the bellows to the tongue? In the two-reed network it is an ideal source of pressure rising in 20 ms, a hypothesis without a measurement. In the one-reed model it is a source of flow with an internal resistance, 
\begin_inset Formula $q=q_{0}-p/R$
\end_inset

, and the audit argues from the playing that this is the right picture: a real bellows, like a real turbine, gives less flow when the pressure rises. The value of 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 decides the amplitude of the note (section on the aperture) and moves the threshold: in one version of the one-reed model the starting pressure of the reference tongue was 23.0 Pa with an ideal source, 25.8 Pa with 
\begin_inset Formula $R=2\times10^{8}$
\end_inset

 and 35.9 Pa with 
\begin_inset Formula $5\times10^{7}$
\end_inset

 Pa s m
\begin_inset Formula $^{-3}$
\end_inset

, a softer source helping the start less, while the choking pressure stayed at 379.8 Pa. These are numbers of a model version whose bass thresholds are known to be far too low; the direction of the effect is what to keep.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The playing pressures of an accordion are of the order of 500 to 3000 Pa, in both signs; the thresholds of the tongues are far below, a few tens to a few hundreds of pascals in the models, where a real instrument asks for roughly a hundred to a thousand all over the keyboard. The bellows also has a compliance of its own and the hand behind it; the audit says both are measurable with a manometer and neither is in a model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
On the bench the bellows is motorised: a stepper axis on a linear table, the only source of air, driven at a set speed or servoed to a pressure, reversing at the ends of its finite stroke so that a measurement is made within one pass, push and draw being the two signs of pressure. The bench does not work at present and has never been rebuilt; the firmware exists and is untested; the flow meter is still there. The same motorised bellows is the modulator of the leak imaging method of the appendix, at 2 to 10 Hz.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): two measurements on one of your bellows, closed on a board with a pressure tap. The leak at 500 Pa, by the gasometer or the flow meter, against the 1 litre per minute of the booklet. And the characteristic of your own arm as a source: pressure against flow at a steady push, through a known orifice, read on the U-tube and the gasometer; its slope is the 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 of the models. Which bellows, which day?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
Timbral elements
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
After the tongue has made the sound, several pieces of the instrument shape it before it reaches the ear. This chapter lists them, says what the physics of this work would predict for each, and admits that none has been measured here.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Grid
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The grille is the perforated screen in front of the pallets of the right hand. The sound of every tongue crosses it. Physically it is a plate with many holes and a thin layer of air behind it: at low frequency it is transparent, since the wavelength is long compared with the holes and the depth; towards high frequencies the holes act as masses of air and the layer behind as a spring, and the grille becomes a filter whose cut-off depends on the fraction of open area and on the depth. Nothing in this work has computed or measured a grille, and no number is given here.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): one note recorded with the grille on and off, same microphone position, same bellows pressure, with the WAV export of the tuner; compare the two spectra above 2 kHz. The difference, if any, is the grille.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Resonance box
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Some instruments put a set of tongues behind a second chamber inside the box, a resonance box, which gives them their rounder sound. Physically it is one more cavity on the road of the air, and the method of chapter 2 applies to it unchanged: mesh its air, compute its modes and the impedance seen from the hole of the block. One caution: a resonance box is many centimetres long, and at one or two kilohertz that is no longer small compared with the wavelength; the lumped network of masses and springs stops being valid there, while the finite element model does not care, having been checked on a duct of 100 mm against the exact formula to a relative error of 
\begin_inset Formula $1.3\times10^{-5}$
\end_inset

. No resonance box has been drawn or computed in this work.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the dimensions of the resonance box of one instrument you can open, and the recording of one tongue through it and outside it, so that the computed modes can be set against the measured spectrum.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Clapets et sourdines
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Pallets and mutes, in French in the title. The pallet is the door of each note, and it has had its say in this book several times: an orifice of guessed dimensions in the network, the only radiator of the model, the mass of air that the model puts in the mechanism of start-up, the last place where the key acts. The register slides, which close whole sets of tongues, are of the same family, and the workshop counts them among the places that leak. A mute is anything, felt, wood or cloth, placed on the road of the sound to soften it: a longer and narrower passage, hence more mass of air and more loss, hence less high frequency and a little less of everything.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
What is said of valves in the chapter on leaks applies to both: their transmittance can be measured in a tube with two or four microphones, the software is ready, the rig is not. What the models predict for the pallet, an optimum of its lift for the ease of the start, is experiment E2 and is unmeasured.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the transmission loss of one register slide, closed, in the two-microphone tube, by the coarse method of transfer.py. And the lift of your pallets at full key, with a feeler gauge: the 4 mm of the model is a guess.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part
Measuring and quantify 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
Ways and measuring protocols
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Nature does not lie, but it speaks softly, and it speaks once. A good measurement is a question asked so clearly that the answer cannot be misread, and asked in a way that can be repeated. This part describes how the reeds of this thesis are listened to, with means that a craftsman can afford: a phone, a sound card, a few small sensors, and protocols written so that the same gesture gives the same number.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One rule guides every protocol: a useful experiment is one whose result would be different if the hypothesis were false. Three times during this work, a measurement contradicted a reasoning that seemed solid. The list of experiments of the repository (research/docs/experiences_a_mener.md) gives, for each one, what is measured, with what, what it unlocks, and the number of the model it must be compared with.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Sound recording using microphone
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The microphone hears what the listener hears: the air chopped by the reed, whose far sound follows the rate of change of the flow (Section Q1). From one recording it gives the pitch, the partials, the beats between reeds, the attack and the level.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Two chains are used. On the research bench, a measurement microphone goes into a 24-bit Behringer interface, which offers three inputs (one stereo pair and one mono). In the workshop, and for many experiments of this thesis, a phone is enough. The tuner of the repository (web/, an application that runs in the browser of a computer or a phone) is the main instrument of measurement. Its precision is better than 0.1 cent, from E0 to C9, and it measures several reeds at the same time: in its register mode, two to five reeds of one note (tremolo, musette, 16 ft, 8 ft and 4 ft ranks), with the beat of each detuned reed. For research it follows each partial up to the eighth at its real frequency, so that the inharmonicity of a reed becomes a visible trace; it draws a phase diagram (for example the intensity against the deviation in cents, which is the frequency-amplitude characteristic of the reed); it measures the response time at each attack (from 10 to 90 per cent); and it watches the bands at 
\begin_inset Formula $f/2$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $3f/2$
\end_inset
, where a period doubling would appear.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A measuring instrument can lie too, and the tuner taught this once. On a phone, through the usual audio path of the browser, the sound was resampled with a ratio that changed by jumps. The whole sound jumped by 
\begin_inset Formula $\pm6$
\end_inset
 cents about every two seconds, even the hum of the mains, which never moves. On the curve this made square steps that the reed never made. The cure was to read the microphone at its own clock, which Chrome and Edge allow. The lesson is worth keeping: when a signal that cannot move seems to move, the instrument is at fault, not the reed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The absolute accuracy depends on the quartz of the sound card, typically 
\begin_inset Formula $\pm20$
\end_inset
 ppm, about 
\begin_inset Formula $\pm0.03$
\end_inset
 cent. It is removed by calibration: play a known reference (a calibrated tuning fork, a generator), enter its frequency, and the tuner measures and corrects the clock error of the card.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For research, a dev mode keeps the raw sound of the microphone and every measurement (about eleven per second: each reed, its target, its frequency, its deviation, its intensity and its partials one to eight), dated on the same clock. One session of up to ten minutes is exported as a single archive with the sound (WAV), the measurements (CSV) and the settings (JSON). Played back, the WAV gives the same measurements to a thousandth of a cent: the recording is a faithful copy of the experiment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A few rules come from the experiments of this thesis. To measure a plucked tongue, hold the phone 10 to 20 cm away and pluck three to five times, one second apart (pince.py). The automatic gain and the noise reduction of a phone do not touch the frequency, but they can falsify the damping, by raising a sound that is dying; two plucks of different strength give a check. To separate the reed (the source) from the resonances of the instrument (the filter), record about ten notes spread over the range, with the same instrument, the same take and a close, dry microphone: with fewer than six notes the resonance is badly located, beyond twelve the gain is marginal, and with one note alone the problem cannot be solved (experiment E10). Absolute levels need a calibrated microphone, which the bench does not have yet; relative levels are reliable if the phone and its distance do not change.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With two microphones in a tube, the electromagnetic exciter of the bench as source, the method of ISO 10534-2 gives the reflection coefficient, the normalised acoustic impedance and the absorption of what closes the tube: a chamber, a pallet, a mute (transfer.py). With four microphones, the same tool gives the transmission loss of a valve or a mute. Two microphones also locate a leak by the delay between them (Appendix Leaks detection).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): which microphone, which interface and which distance are used for the reference recordings of the thesis, and in which room, so that all the recordings can be compared.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Sound recording using accelerometers
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A microphone listens to the air. An accelerometer listens to the wood and the metal: the reed plate, the reed block, the soundboard, the box. It tells what vibrates, where the microphone only tells what radiates. Comparing the two is how one can find whether a part of the instrument sings with the reeds or stays silent.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The means are cheap: a light piezoelectric accelerometer of a few euros, fixed on the plate, read on the third input of the Behringer interface (research/docs/mesures_par_chapitre.md). The earlier measurement campaigns already recorded accelerations: the file d_Mesures_Accelerations.npy (384 MB, on the Drive, read with banc_recherche.campaigns.load_npy without loading it in memory) waits to be analysed (research/docs/DATA.md).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One precaution is physical. A sensor adds its own mass to the part it sits on, and a heavier part vibrates at lower frequencies. On a light reed plate, a few grams can shift a resonance. The sensor must therefore be as light as possible, and its effect checked once: the same resonance measured with the microphone, with and without the sensor in place. The cable must hang free, without touching the instrument.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With a small impact hammer, the accelerometer, or the microphone, gives the frequency response of a grid or of the box, the ratio of the vibration to the force of the tap, from which their modes are read (a proposal, not built yet).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): which accelerometer was used in the earlier campaigns (model, mass, where and how it was fixed), and what these recordings showed, if they were looked at.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
Instrumented accordion for 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

In-vivo
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 measurements
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
On the bench a reed lives alone, at a pressure chosen by the experimenter. In the hands of a musician, everything moves at once: the arm drives the bellows, the fingers open and close the pallets, the pressure rises and falls within a phrase, and several notes share the air. To measure in vivo is to measure while someone plays. Nothing of this has been built yet; this chapter describes what the repository already allows and what it proposes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Sensors and their place
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Four quantities would describe the playing: the pressure in the bellows, the pressure in a few reed chambers, the position and the direction of the bellows, and the sound. Micro pressure sensors fit in a chamber; the bellows position can be read by a cord sensor or by the angle of the frame; a microphone records the sound. A small ESP32 board would carry the sensors and send their readings wirelessly, as the firmware of the tuning bench already does: it streams time-stamped readings about twenty times per second over WiFi to the tuner, and can take bursts of pressure, flow and temperature up to 150 readings per second. The time stamp is the bridge between the sensors and the sound, because the sensors and the microphone do not share the same clock. For the keyboard, a load cell (HX711) and an angle sensor (AS5600) would give the force and the travel of a key.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The choice of the pressure sensor matters. An absolute sensor, like the BMP280 of the bench firmware, reads the whole atmospheric pressure (about 101 kPa) and the small overpressure on top of it; with such a full scale, its noise can reach several pascals. The thresholds of reeds can be a few tens of pascals. A differential sensor, which reads the pressure relative to the room, like the XGZP6847D of the 2023 bench (range probably 4 to 8 kPa), suits these small pressures better. Whatever the sensor, its scale is checked once against a transparent U-tube filled with water: 1 mm of difference between the two water levels is 9.81 Pa. If the two readings differ by more than 5 per cent, the scale factor of the sensor is corrected.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
What an instrumented accordion would answer
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The real pressures of playing, from pianissimo to fortissimo, note by note, to compare with the starting and choking pressures of the reeds (Part Reeds).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The bellows as a source: its pressure-flow characteristic in the hand of a player, the parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset
 of question Q4.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The bellow shake: the pressure and the pitch during a fast push-pull of the bellows.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The drift of pitch within real phrases, where the models predict that the note goes down when the player pushes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): which instrument would be instrumented first, which notes, and which sensors you already have.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
Experiment table for 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

In-vitro
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 measurement
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In vitro, in the glass: the reed is taken out of the instrument and studied alone, under conditions that the experimenter holds still. This chapter describes the experiment table of the thesis: why it exists, what it measures and moves, how it serves the tuner, and the small tools around it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Why this table ?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A musician changes ten things at once. A model needs to know what one change does. The experiment table exists to ask one question at a time: the same reed, the same pressure, the same distance to the microphone, and one factor changed. It makes the experiments repeatable, and it makes the designs of experiments possible, where several factors are changed in a planned order so that the effect of each one, and of their interactions, can be separated (banc_recherche/doe.py, doe_analysis).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Two benches live in the repository. The research bench runs on a computer (banc_recherche, with a graphical interface and a command line): the Behringer interface, the electromagnetic exciter of the reed, and a table moved by a stepper motor. The tuning bench is driven by an ESP32-S3 board (firmware/): a motorised bellows, electromagnets on the buttons, and pressure, flow and temperature sensors. Their ancestors are kept: a Pyboard tuning table of 2021, the computer scripts of 2022 and 2023, and a pressure bench of 2023 (research/legacy).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Their state must be said plainly. The firmware of the tuning bench is a clean rewrite of the old Pyboard script, but it has never been tested on the hardware, and the motorised bench has not been rebuilt since. The protocol of research/docs/protocole_recalage_experimental.md gives the steps to bring it back, one at a time, with the actuators disconnected first.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The table already has a history of planned experiments. In 2018, a design of experiments on the soundboard of the Accordina and the Octodyna varied six geometric factors and measured pressure and flow. Between 2020 and 2022, a full factorial plan of 17 runs (
\begin_inset Formula $2^{4}$
\end_inset
 runs and one centre point, in random order) was prepared to study the coupling between reed and cavity under magnetic excitation, with four factors: the height under the plate on the tongue side (10 to 20 mm), the width of the hole in the heel (3 to 15 mm), the thickness of the heel (3 to 50 mm) and the angle between reed and air inlet (0 to 90 degrees). Its response columns are empty: the plan was not carried out. It can be run as it is with the tools of the repository (research/legacy/plans-experience-2018-2022).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): which of the two benches you want to bring back first, and whether the 17-run plan of 2020 to 2022 is still the right first campaign.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Sensors & actuator
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The table moves air and buttons, and it reads what happens. On the side of the air: a motorised bellows driven by a stepper motor, which is the only source of air of the tuning bench. It can run at a set speed, hold a set pressure by adjusting its speed and reversing by itself at the ends of its travel, or make a single clean stroke for a measurement. A solenoid valve opens or closes the circuit. A screw sets the section of the air passage (its area is the travel in millimetres times 15 mm), and a second axis sets the angle of the pallet.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
On the side of the sensors: the pressure and the temperature (BMP280, or the XGZP6847D of 2023), the flow (Sensirion SFM3000), and a displacement sensor moved along the reed to read its profile at rest (any sensor will do, from a dial gauge to a photograph: Section Static shape of free reeds reeds). To play notes, a matrix of electromagnets presses 24 buttons of the left hand and 47 of the right hand, held with little power by pulse-width modulation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
On the research bench, the electromagnetic exciter makes the tongue vibrate without air: a sweep gives its resonances; a short excitation followed by silence gives its ring-down, from which the Matrix Pencil method 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
before ""
key "hua1990matrixpencil"
literal "true"

\end_inset
, already in the tuner, extracts the frequency and the damping of each mode.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The tuning bench is driven by short text commands (for example HOME, TARE, PRESSURE, STROKE, SECTION, CLAP, PRESS, VALVE, ACQUIRE, LEAKTEST, SCAN, STOP) and answers with time-stamped readings. STOP puts every actuator in a safe state.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Two checks wait before the first measurement. The driver of the flow sensor uses an offset of 32 768 and a scale of 140; the datasheet of the exact SFM3000 model must confirm them, otherwise the flow at rest will not read zero. And the pressure sensor of 2023 is described in one document as absolute and in another as differential: the reference written on the sensor decides.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the exact reference written on the pressure sensor (XGZP6847 or XGZP6847D) and the datasheet of your SFM3000.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Practical for tuning
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The table also serves the daily work of the tuner. The tuner of the repository has a tab that connects to the bench, wirelessly or by cable, and replays a design of experiments: for each point it sets the section, the pressure and the pallet, presses a button, and records, while the browser measures the pitch, the attack, the resonances and the impedance 
\begin_inset Formula $p/q$
\end_inset
. Each pneumatic point is thus tied to its acoustic answer.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
At the bench, the strobe of the tuner shows each reed at once, in the manner of a Peterson strobe, and says what to do with the tongue: too high, scrape towards the heel to lower it; too low, scrape towards the tip to raise it. Four lights come on as the reed approaches its target: 5, 1, 0.3 and 0.1 cent. Each band of the strobe folds the sound on the period of one partial: a reed in tune stands still; a reed out of tune but harmonic slides as a block; an inharmonic partial slides under the others and deforms the pattern.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the voices of a note, the register mode measures two to five reeds together without blocking any of them, and shows the beat of each detuned reed. A list of beats, an exponential curve with corrections note by note, defines the target tremolo, and the beats measured on one instrument can be copied as the target of another. Pushing and pulling are measured and stored separately: at each reversal of the bellows the sound passes through a silence, and the measurement starts again from zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The table also checks the joints. The leak test of the tuning table, formalised in the protocol: at a fixed pressure (for example 500 Pa), read the flow with the pallet free (
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{1}$
\end_inset
), then pressed shut (
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{2}$
\end_inset
), three times; the joint of this pallet leaks if 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{1}-Q_{2}$
\end_inset
 is larger than three times the spread of the flow at rest (Appendix Leaks detection).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One question links this section to Part Reeds: at which pressure is a reed tuned? If the pitch goes down when the player pushes, as every model of this thesis predicts, a reed tuned at one pressure is out of tune at the others.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): at which pressure, or with which bellows gesture, do you tune; and is it the same for bass and treble reeds?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Accessories
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Around the table, small tools do much of the work, and most of them cost almost nothing:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
a transparent U-tube with water and a ruler: the reference of pressure (1 mm of water is 9.81 Pa);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
feeler gauges from 0.05 to 1 mm, a dial gauge reading to 0.01 mm, a flat glass plate or a marble, and a lamp: the lift of the tongue and the gaps;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
a calliper and, if possible, a micrometer: the dimensions and thicknesses of the tongues;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
a precision scale: the density of the steel and the masses;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
a phone: sound, and slow-motion video at 240 frames per second to watch a bass tongue move;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
incense, for the smoke that shows a leak; tape and paper, to block a reed; modelling clay, to change the volume of a chamber; a syringe of water, to measure the volume of a chamber;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
blanking plates and sealing jigs, which close a part during a leak test.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the list of these tools that you already own, so that the protocols ask for nothing new.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part
Appendix
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
Leaks detection
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A leak is air that escapes without making music. It is invisible and almost silent, and yet it changes everything. It steals pressure before the note: the attack is soft, and a pianissimo starts badly. Every leak eats air, so the bellows must move more for the same sound and the player tires faster. And a pallet that leaks can make a neighbouring note sound when nobody plays it: a ghost note. This appendix gathers the ways to measure a leak and to find it, from the gestures of the workshop to methods that make a leak sing and become visible.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
How big is a leak?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A leak is measured as a flow, in litres per minute, at a given pressure: 500 Pa, about 5 cm of water, the pressure of a medium nuance. A leak can also be given as an equivalent hole: the round hole that would let the same flow through. The law of a small sharp-edged hole links the two: 
\begin_inset Formula $q=C_{d}\,S\,\sqrt{2\Delta p/\rho}$
\end_inset
, where 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset
 is the area of the hole, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta p$
\end_inset
 the pressure difference, 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset
 the density of air and 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{d}\approx0.61$
\end_inset
 the contraction of the jet. Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:trou-equivalent"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset
 gives a few values.
\end_layout

