\begin_layout Standard
The bench does the same with a sensor. With the valve closed and the bellows stopped, the pressure in a closed volume falls as the air leaks out. For a small leak it falls like 
\begin_inset Formula $p(t)=p_{0}\,e^{-t/\tau}$
\end_inset
: the time constant 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau$
\end_inset
 measures the tightness, and the volume divided by 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau$
\end_inset
 gives the conductance of the leak (command LEAKTEST of the firmware, analysis by banc_recherche/leak.py).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A third global method, proposed, listens to the resonances of the closed instrument: a leak damps the air cavity, so the quality factor 
\begin_inset Formula $Q$
\end_inset
 of its resonances drops. Compared with a sealed reference, a sine sweep and a ring-down measure the total leak and its spectral signature, without locating it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Finding one leak: the gestures of the workshop
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The tuning-table test finds the leak of one pallet: the flow with the pallet free, then pressed shut (Section Practical for tuning). Other gestures need no sensor at all. A water gasometer, an upside-down box floating in a bucket and weighted, connected by a pipe to the part under test, sinks when the part leaks; its speed is the leak flow divided by the area of the box (1 L/min lowers a box of 150 cm
\begin_inset Formula $^{2}$
\end_inset
 by 6.7 cm per minute). A bell, a small pot with a foam rim, is placed over one closed pallet and tests it alone. Incense smoke, moved slowly along the joints, bends where the air escapes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Two safety rules go with these gestures: never a flame to look for a leak (celluloid, wood and glue burn), and no soapy water on wood, leather or card. And before trusting a measurement, test the bench itself: a plate laid on a sheet of glass must not leak; then check the bench on reference leaks, a plate drilled with holes of 0.3, 0.5 and 1 mm, whose flows are known from Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:trou-equivalent"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Making a leak sing
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A jet of air through a small hole becomes turbulent and hisses, with a noise that rises high, up to the ultrasound, where the voice of the reeds and the noise of the room are weak. The hiss is still lost in the noise of a room. The method proposed in this thesis makes it stand out by giving it a rhythm. The bellows modulates the pressure slowly, as a sine at a known frequency 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{\mathrm{mod}}$
\end_inset
 of 2 to 10 Hz. Every leak then hisses louder and softer at 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{\mathrm{mod}}$
\end_inset
. A cheap microphone, moved over the surface, records; the program keeps the band of the hiss (about 4 to 20 kHz), takes its envelope, and keeps only what beats exactly at 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{\mathrm{mod}}$
\end_inset
, with the right phase. This is synchronous detection, the lock-in of the physics laboratory: everything that does not beat at our rhythm is rejected, and the leak appears on a map (banc_recherche/leak.py: lockin, acoustic_leak_strength, leak_map).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Two microphones can also locate a leak without moving: its hiss reaches them with a small delay that depends on its position, and the cross-correlation of the two signals gives this delay (leak.tdoa_delay).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Making a leak visible
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Two further methods are proposed and not built yet. The first, background-oriented schlieren, films a printed speckled background through the jet: air of a different density bends the light a little, and a program that compares the images reveals the invisible jet, all the more if the air is warm and humid, like a breath. The second fills the bellows with carbon dioxide and scans the surface with a small gas sensor of a few euros: the concentration rises at the leaks.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally, each reed signs its own leak. A leak near a reed steals part of its air: the reed starts later, sounds softer and goes out of tune in a characteristic way. The tuner already measures the deviation, the level and the response time of each reed; a local leak leaves its trace there, without any scanning microphone.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Proposed acceptance figures
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The workshop handbook of Ewen's accordion-making course proposes the following targets, all at 500 Pa. They are proposals, not yet measured on his instruments, and they are to be calibrated on two of them:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
soundboard and pallets: less than 0.02 L/min per pallet, and no pallet lifts below 1.5 times the highest playing pressure;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
bellows alone: less than 1 L/min;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
reed block glued, bare or with its reeds (slots taped): less than 0.05 L/min;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
closed case: less than 0.5 L/min;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
whole instrument: a drop time to be set by Ewen.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the leak and the drop time measured on two of your instruments, to turn these proposals into figures; and the area of the box of your gasometer.
\end_layout

